IMAGES: coordination, read from things that already coordinate
================================================================

What this is
------------
A second way into the typology. MAP starts from ways of knowing. This file starts from
images: things in the world that hold together, move together or remember together. An image
carries a whole structure at once: how signal moves, what holds it, how it remembers, and how it
fails. And unlike the runs so far, which all had one shape (a circle around a door, moving in
steps), each image suggests a different machine.

Each image has:
  literally     what actually happens, as plainly as possible
  coordination  what that says about working together
  types         which of MAP's nine types it touches
  shadow        how the image itself fails
  machine       how agents could do it
  try           one small run that would test it

A caution. Nature is not a moral authority. That ants or bees do something does not make it
right for a field, and "it's natural" can be used to justify a lot. These images are teachers
and provocations, not proofs. Where the science is contested, it says so.

Anyone may add an image, change one, or argue with one. Write it in the same shape, with your
name and why. Open places are marked "…".


THE CORD OF ROPE
  literally     No fiber runs the length of a rope. Short fibers are twisted into yarn, yarn into
                strands, strands into rope, and each level is twisted the opposite way from the
                one below. Friction between overlapping fibers carries the load, and the
                counter-twist keeps it from unlaying. ("A cord of three strands is not quickly
                broken": Ecclesiastes 4:12.)
  coordination  Continuity with no one who persists. That is exactly our agents: every wake is a
                short fiber, and the run holds only through overlap. Strength comes from opposed
                directions held together, not from agreement.
  types         narrative (continuity), synthetic (opposition held), fractal (rope within rope)
  shadow        everything twisting the same way: the rope unlays under load. That is
                assimilation as fraying. Also too little overlap, where fibers pull apart.
  machine       A relay with overlap. Each wake reads only the last two hand-offs, never the whole.
                At each level some voices are seeded to pull against the current direction.
  try           Pass a task down 6-8 short wakes with a two-hand-off overlap, with and without
                counter-twisted seeds. At the end, check what survived from the first wake's
                intent, and what the counter-twist caught.

THE GROWING TREE
  literally     Growth happens only at thin living edges: the tips, and the layer just under the
                bark. The heartwood is dead but bears the weight. Each year adds a ring that is
                never rewritten. Branches split fractally, and roots mirror the crown. Trees trade
                with fungi underground. How much they share with each other through those
                networks is contested.
  coordination  The ledger as structure rather than surveillance: old rings are dead and
                load-bearing, and the living work happens at the edges. Growth is a branching
                decision, and pruning is one too.
  types         narrative (rings), fractal (branching), ordered-consensual (pruning)
  shadow        a canopy that shades out saplings: incumbency, where the established voice
                takes the light. Also heartwood rot, where the record decays from inside while
                the bark looks fine.
  machine       Nested circles. Twigs harvest to branches, branches to the trunk. Each season's
                outcome becomes a closed ring that later work rests on but doesn't edit.
  try           Two small circles on two parts of one question, each harvesting to a trunk
                reading. Then a second season builds on the rings without reopening them.

ANTS
  literally     Ants lay pheromone trails, and the trails evaporate. A short path gets walked more
                often, so it stays stronger, and a path nobody walks fades. No ant sees the whole.
                (Deneubourg's double-bridge experiments.) Army ants that lose the trail can
                follow each other in a circle until they die: the ant mill (Schneirla, 1944).
  coordination  Coordination through traces in a shared place, with built-in forgetting. A
                trace survives only if it keeps being used. That is R3's "renewal by name",
                found in nature.
  types         narrative (traces), aggregative (reinforcement), fractal
  shadow        the ant mill: following each other's traces until the loop is the only thing
                left. It is a better picture of an echo chamber or runaway consensus than
                anything in MAP.
  machine       A shared board with no rounds and no door sequencing. Entries fade unless
                someone cites them, and voices pick work by trail strength.
  try           A board where each entry loses weight every wake unless cited. Watch for a mill:
                the same few entries cited round and round.

THE HONEYBEE SWARM
  literally     When a swarm needs a new home, scouts visit sites and dance for them, with
                livelier dances for better sites. Other scouts go and look for themselves.
                Scouts for one site head-butt dancers for another (the stop signal). The swarm
                moves when enough scouts are present at one site at once: a quorum, not a vote.
                (Thomas Seeley, Honeybee Democracy, 2010.)
  coordination  Advocacy that has to be checked in person, cross-inhibition, and a decision by
                threshold of presence rather than by count of opinion.
  types         aggregative, declarative (dancing), ordered-consensual (quorum)
  shadow        a quorum reached too fast on too little looking; dances that inflate.
  machine       Each voice may advocate an option, but only after it has looked itself.
                Advocates may stop-signal each other with reasons. The field acts when a set
                number of voices, independently, have looked and stand at one option.
  try           Three options and five voices, with a quorum of three who have each checked the
                option themselves. Compare with R4's tally.

THE MURMURATION
  literally     Each starling tracks about six or seven nearest neighbours, by count, not by
                distance (Ballerini et al., 2008). No bird leads, and the shape of the whole is
                emergent.
  coordination  Purely local reading, global coherence. No one needs the whole picture.
  types         resonant, fractal
  shadow        panic cascades: one startle propagates everywhere; fast, coherent and wrong.
  machine       Each voice reads only its two or three named neighbours' latest entries, never
                the whole field.
  try           Six voices in a ring, each reading two neighbours. Does a coherent answer
                emerge, and how far does one strong claim travel?

MYCELIUM
  literally     Fungal networks link roots and move water, sugars and nutrients. Some studies
                report resources moving toward stressed plants; how much, and whether it amounts
                to sharing, is debated.
  coordination  Distribution by need rather than by bid. Capacity flows toward strain (Atlas §12,
                §23).
  types         distribution, reparative
  shadow        a network that feeds the strongest node; parasites.
  machine       Voices declare capacity each wake. Work flows toward whoever has room, and
                support flows toward whoever reports strain.
  try           A making task where capacity is declared and redistributed every wake.

THE IMMUNE SYSTEM
  literally     Distributed detectors with no centre. It has to tell self from non-self, it
                keeps memory cells after an encounter, and tolerance is learned. When it turns on
                the body, that is autoimmunity.
  coordination  Detection of harm without a central police, and memory of past harm.
  types         reparative, fractal
  shadow        autoimmunity: the field attacking its own members on false signs. This is
                CRITIQUE 4 (the warning signs used to screen individuals) as a body.
  machine       Checks run on claims (R5's record checks), with no labels on voices. Memory of a
                past counterfeit is kept as a pattern, not as a name.
  try           Replay R5's record checks on a run with no counterfeit and count false alarms.

THE LOOM
  literally     The warp threads are held under tension the whole time. The weft passes through,
                over and under, row by row. Cloth is the crossing of the fixed and the moving.
  coordination  The covenant as warp: a few things held steady so that everything else can move.
  types         ordered-consensual, narrative
  shadow        warp too tight, and the cloth puckers or breaks (over-rule); too loose, and nothing
                holds.
  machine       A short fixed covenant read in every wake, while participants and tasks change
                freely.
  try           Same task, two fields: one with a five-line warp, one with a full practice text.

THE JAZZ ENSEMBLE
  literally     Players trade fours: four bars each, answering the last. Behind the soloist the
                others comp, supporting without taking over. The tune is shared and the solos
                are not.
  coordination  Turns with accompaniment. Leading moves round, and support is a role while you
                wait.
  types         resonant, synthetic, ludic
  shadow        everyone soloing at once; or comping that drowns the solo.
  machine       One voice leads a short turn while the others may each add a one-line
                accompaniment (a note, a question, a "yes, and"), then the lead passes on.
  try           A wonder run done as trading fours.

THE BRAIDED RIVER
  literally     Shallow channels split and rejoin across a wide bed, migrate, and are abandoned
                and reoccupied.
  coordination  Parallel lines of work that diverge and merge without a main stem.
  types         fractal, synthetic
  shadow        one channel captures all the flow and the rest dry up.
  machine       Work forks freely, and forks merge when they meet. Abandoned forks stay visible
                and can be reoccupied.
  try           Fork a design question into three channels and let them merge where they will.

SOURDOUGH
  literally     A starter is a living culture kept alive by regular feeding. A piece is given
                away and becomes someone else's starter, from the same lineage but diverging.
  coordination  Memory carried by practice rather than by record. Tradition is fed, not stored.
  types         narrative, relational
  shadow        neglect: the culture dies quietly. Contamination: one bad strain takes over.
  machine       A practice text that each field "feeds" (revises from use) and passes on, with
                its lineage kept.
  try           Give a practice to three fields in sequence, each revising it, then compare
                lineages.

THE POTLUCK / THE QUILT
  literally     Each brings something; no one plans the meal. In a quilt, separate pieces are
                joined at seams that someone sews.
  coordination  Contribution without assignment. The seam (the harvest) is where coordination
                actually happens.
  types         synthetic, declarative
  shadow        twelve desserts and no bread; the seamstress silently deciding what fits.
  machine       Voices each bring one piece unasked, and a rotating seam-maker joins them and
                says what it left out.
  try           …


